the best buffer is only about 10–15 units. A 1.10 factor adds about 54 extra units over the period, and the extra purchases and storage cost more than the shortages they prevent. Candidate 1 comes out roughly 100–150 better than the 1.10 plans on this good.\n  - Good 1: the best level is about 1.08–1.10. A 1.15 factor costs about 150 more than optimal, and 1.25–1.30 costs much more. Candidate 1 is slightly below the optimum, but the loss is small.\n- **Batching does not pay off.** Ordering every 2 days (candidates 0 and 7) saves 11 order days, which is 363. But it leaves an extra day's demand in stock on alternate nights. Over the 11 two-day cycles that is about 395 in storage for good 0 and about 60 for good 1, roughly 455 in total. That is more than the saving. Ordering every 3 days (candidate 6) saves 594 but adds roughly 800 in storage for good 0. It is also tight on cash around day 6: about 1780 available against 1695 needed, so a weak demand stretch could get the order cut.\n\n**Robustness to random demand**\nCandidate 1 runs close to zero net drift, so it loses only a moderate amount to random shortages and avoids systematic overstock. The plans with factors of 1.15–1.30 lose money steadily whichever way the actual demand moves.\n\n**Ranking:** 1 > 3 ≈ 0 > 4 > 6 > 5 > 7 ≈ 2."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 355, "cache_read_input_tokens": 5735, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 355}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-26T09:19:37.129Z", "request_id": "req_011CgQbbCUQpZ936qwrY27Zp"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", 